      "reference": {
        "title": "Benign & inflammatory bowel resection reference gallery",
        "intro": "Diagnostic criteria, open-licence photomicrographs and literature links for diverticular disease, IBD (UC vs Crohn disease), ischaemic enterocolitis, gastrointestinal amyloidosis and mimics.",
        "columns": 2,
        "groups": [
          {
            "heading": "Diverticular disease",
            "points": [
              "False (pseudodiverticula) herniations of mucosa and submucosa through the muscularis propria at vasa recta entry points, accompanied by muscularis propria hypertrophy",
              "Acute diverticulitis: active neutrophilic inflammation, ulceration, suppurative peridiverticulitis, pericolic abscess or fibrinous serositis",
              "Segmental colitis associated with diverticulosis (SCAD): chronic active colitis confined to the interdiverticular mucosa (sparing rectum and proximal colon)"
            ],
            "links": [
              {
                "label": "PathologyOutlines — Colonic diverticulosis & diverticulitis",
                "url": "https://www.pathologyoutlines.com/topic/colondiverticulosis.html"
              },
              {
                "label": "PMC — Colonic diverticular pathology overview",
                "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7947368/"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "heading": "Inflammatory bowel disease (UC vs Crohn disease)",
            "points": [
              "Ulcerative colitis: continuous colorectal involvement extending proximally from the rectum; inflammation limited to mucosa and superficial submucosa (except fulminant colitis/toxic megacolon); marked crypt architectural distortion, basal plasmacytosis and Paneth cell metaplasia",
              "Crohn disease: segmental skip lesions (any GI site, commonly terminal ileum/ileocaecal); transmural lymphoid aggregates (rosary-bead pattern), knife-like fissuring ulcers, sinus tracts/fistulae, creeping fat and non-necrotising epithelioid granulomas",
              "Always screen multiple blocks for IBD-associated dysplasia (conventional, hypermucinous, serrated, crypt cell) and invasive adenocarcinoma"
            ],
            "links": [
              {
                "label": "JPTM (2021) — Dysplastic Subtypes in Inflammatory Bowel Disease (Choi)",
                "url": "https://www.jpatholtm.org/upload/pdf/jptm_v55n2_full.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "heading": "Ischaemia / infarction",
            "points": [
              "Acute ischaemic enterocolitis: superficial mucosal coagulative necrosis with withered/atrophic ('ghost') crypts, lamina propria hyalinisation and haemorrhage, capillary fibrin thrombi and overlying pseudomembranes",
              "Transmural infarction / gangrene: full-thickness coagulative necrosis involving muscularis propria and serosa with acute serositis; inspect mesenteric vessels for thrombus, embolus, atherosclerosis or vasculitis",
              "Chronic ischaemia: submucosal fibrosis, haemosiderin-laden macrophages, mucosal atrophy and stricturing; assess both resection margins for viability"
            ],
            "links": [
              {
                "label": "PathologyOutlines — Ischaemic colitis",
                "url": "https://pathologyoutlinesblog.wordpress.com/tag/ischemic-colitis/"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "heading": "Gastrointestinal amyloidosis",
            "points": [
              "Extracellular acellular, amorphous eosinophilic deposits in submucosal vessel walls, lamina propria, muscularis mucosae and/or muscularis propria (may cause ischaemia, ulceration or pseudo-obstruction)",
              "Congo red positive (salmon-pink on brightfield) with characteristic apple-green birefringence under polarised light; always recommend amyloid subtyping (AA vs AL vs ATTR by IHC or mass spectrometry)"
            ],
            "images": [
              {
                "src": "assets/reference/heart_explant/amyloid_he.jpg",
                "caption": "Amyloidosis reference (H&E) — extracellular amorphous eosinophilic amyloid deposits.",
                "credit": "Nephron (Wikimedia Commons)",
                "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
                "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cardiac_amyloidosis_high_mag_he.jpg"
              },
              {
                "src": "assets/reference/heart_explant/amyloid_congo.jpg",
                "caption": "Amyloidosis reference (Congo red) — salmon-pink amyloid deposits (apple-green birefringence under polarised light).",
                "credit": "Nephron (Wikimedia Commons)",
                "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
                "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cardiac_amyloidosis_intermed_mag.jpg"
              }
            ],
            "links": [
              {
                "label": "PathologyOutlines — Colonic amyloidosis",
                "url": "https://www.pathologyoutlines.com/topic/colonamyloidosis.html"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "heading": "Radiation, CMV & other mimics",
            "points": [
              "Radiation enterocolitis: hyalinised lamina propria and submucosa, bizarre atypical radiation fibroblasts, telangiectatic vessels and obliterative endarteritis",
              "CMV enterocolitis: enlarged endothelial and stromal cells with basophilic intranuclear ('owl's eye') and cytoplasmic inclusions; confirm with CMV immunohistochemistry",
              "Meckel diverticulum: true diverticulum containing all layers of bowel wall; document heterotopic gastric or pancreatic tissue and any adjacent peptic ulceration"
            ]
          }
        ],
        "note": "Reference criteria and open-access links for benign and inflammatory bowel resections; nothing here prints."
      }
    },
